### Remote Attestation Security Analysis (`draft-ietf-lake-ra`)
* **Presenter:** Usama Mansoor
* **Slides:** [11-Vulnerabilities in lake-ra-06](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lake-10-vulnerabilities-in-lake-ra-06-00)
* **Discussion:**
  * Usama Mansoor presented claims that `draft-ietf-lake-ra-06` evidence binding lacks correlation with application traffic keys (Level 3 binding), drawing analogies from earlier TLS attestation draft analysis.
  * Yushun Li, Nathaniel Ritz, Ionuț Mihalcea, and chairs noted that formal analysis presented at the May 2026 interim verified strong session binding, and that absence of Level 3 binding in symbolic models does not translate to an actionable protocol attack.
  * Usama Mansoor requested access to open-source implementation code to demonstrate vulnerabilities.
  * Mališa Vučinić and Francesc-Xavier Aguiló clarified that the IETF standardizes protocol specifications rather than maintaining specific research implementations, and encouraged open publication of formal models and code.
  * Scott Fluhrer (AD) noted that this topic has been extensively debated across multiple meetings without consensus on the vulnerability claims, and recommended concluding this debate in working group sessions and taking technical/peer-reviewed arguments to academic forums or offline channels.

